    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Delhi was built, destroyed and rebuilt several times, and tradition counts seven cities on its site, from the Tomara fort of 1052 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>’s Shahjahanabad.</li><li>It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> from 1206 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> from 1638, and it was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1398 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1739.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> moved its Indian capital to Delhi in 1911, and after Partition in 1947 the city changed from a Mughal city to a Punjabi one.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purana-qila">Purana Qila</a>, which tradition identifies with the Indraprastha of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, have found painted grey pottery of the 11th century BCE but no sign of a built environment or fortifications.</p></aside>
    </section>

