    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1052</span><span class="rf-text">Anang Pal of the Tomara dynasty builds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-kot">Lal Kot</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1206</span><span class="rf-text">Qutb-ud-din Aibak founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> with Delhi as its capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1398</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacks Delhi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1638</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> makes Shahjahanabad the Mughal capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1739</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> sacks and loots the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1857</span><span class="rf-text">Rebels hold Delhi until British troops retake it in September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">The capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> is announced to be moving to Delhi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> becomes the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lal-kot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lal-kot.jpg" alt="Lal Kot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lal-kot">Lal Kot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Outer_Wall_of_Lal_kot_and_Rai_Pithora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roboture, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Delhi lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> river, chiefly on its right bank, between the Yamuna floodplains and the Delhi Ridge, which runs out of the Aravalli Range. Carbon dating places an Ochre Coloured Pottery culture in the area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a> from about 2000 BCE, and people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture</a>, which corresponds to the Vedic period, lived in the region around 1200 BCE.</p><p>A long tradition identifies the place with Indraprastha, the city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, which was composed between about 400 BCE and 300 CE but describes an earlier time. The topography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purana-qila">Purana Qila</a> matches the epic’s description, and a village called Indarpat survived inside the fort into the early 20th century. Excavations, however, have found painted grey pottery of the 11th century BCE and no sign of a built environment or fortifications. Buddhist literature of the Mauryan period called the city Indapatta, and an inscription of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> was found near Srinivaspuri in 1966.</p><p>There are several legends of the name Delhi, among them a king called Dhillu or Dilu who built a city here in 50 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> word dhili (loose). The Tomara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> king Anang Pal built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-kot">Lal Kot</a> and several temples in 1052 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> later took Lal Kot and renamed it Qila Rai Pithora.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="Shah Jahan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Jujhar_Singh_Bundela_Kneels_in_Submission_to_Shah_Jahan%27,_painted_by_Bichitr,_c._1630,_Chester_Beatty_Library_(cropped2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bichitr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Delhi grew as a series of cities, traditionally counted as seven. After 1206 Qutb-ud-din Aibak began the Qutub Minar and the Quwwat-al-Islam mosque, the earliest surviving mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The tower, 72.5 m high, was finished in the 13th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>. The sultans who followed built a sequence of forts and townships, and the sultanate reached its greatest extent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> (1325 to 1351). In the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> brought two sandstone pillars inscribed with the edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> to the city.</p><p>In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> built the sixth city and the fort known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purana-qila">Purana Qila</a>, on a site settled since ancient times. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> ruled at first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, Sikri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>’s Tomb in Delhi. The largest change came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> built Shahjahanabad, the seventh city, now the Old City, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a> and the Jama Masjid. The mosque was built in 1656 and is described as the largest in India. Shah Jahan made the city his capital from 1638. Chandni Chowk, a 17th-century market, is known for jewellery and zari saris, and the city’s crafts include zardozi, embroidery in gold thread, and meenakari, enamelling.</p><p>The 18th century added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jantar-mantar">Jantar Mantar</a>, an astronomical observatory. After 1911 the British built another city to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iltutmish.jpg" alt="Iltutmish" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Sultanates._Delhi._Shams_al-D%C4%ABn_Iltutmish._AH_607-633_AD_1210-1235._AR_Tanka_(25mm,_11.07_g,_6h)._Sind_type.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The recorded history of Delhi begins with the Tomara dynasty, placed by one reckoning from 736 to 1193. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> held the city until Prithviraj III was defeated by Muhammad Ghori in 1192 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-tarain">Second Battle of Tarain</a>. In 1206 Qutb-ud-din Aibak, a former slave who had risen to be a general and a governor, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and its Mamluk dynasty. Five dynasties held Delhi as capital: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, the Khaljis (from 1290), the Tughluqs, the Sayyids (1414 to 1451) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodis</a> (1451 to 1526). Razia, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>, became Sultana of Delhi on his death.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated the last Lodi sultan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526, and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> out, and his son Islam Shah ruled from Delhi. In 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>’s army at Delhi and took the throne, but lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a>. Akbar ruled from Agra. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> returned the capital to Delhi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> crowned himself there in 1658.</p><p>After 1707 the empire weakened. A treaty of 1752 made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> protectors of the Mughal throne, and the city was taken in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> (1753), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> (1757 and 1761), the Marathas and, briefly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> in 1783. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> defeated the Marathas in 1803, and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II remained a figurehead. Direct British rule followed the rebellion of 1857.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau.jpg" alt="Amir Khusrau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amir_Khusro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous artist from Bokhara, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Delhi was a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> under the sultans, and the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamuddin-auliya">Nizamuddin Auliya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a> are prominently associated with its Sufi tradition and with qawwali music. The Khariboli dialect of Delhi was part of a linguistic development that gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and later to Modern Standard Hindi. Mir Taqi Mir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-ghalib">Mirza Ghalib</a> are counted among the major Urdu poets of the city, and Dard, Dagh and Zauq lived there and sought the emperor’s patronage.</p><p>Its monuments record the faiths of its rulers. Qutb-ud-din Aibak is said to have destroyed twenty-seven Jain temples in the Qutb complex and to have used their carved pillars and building material for the mosque, and many of the pillars can still be seen. Some palatial residences, the havelis, stand in the Old City.</p><p>The city changed in population as well as in buildings. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> in 1947 about 500,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> refugees, mainly from West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, came to Delhi, and about 300,000 of the city’s Muslim residents went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It has been described as a change from a Mughal city to a Punjabi one, with the loss of two-thirds of its Muslim residents, in part through pressure from the arriving refugees. Hindi is now the most spoken language, and Urdu and Punjabi are additional official languages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shah.jpg" alt="Nader Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Nader_Shah._Artist_unknown,_created_in_ca._1740_in_Iran_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Delhi was sacked more than once. In 1398 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on the pretext that the sultans of Delhi were too tolerant of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> subjects. He defeated the army of Nasiruddin Mahmud of the Tughluq dynasty and entered Delhi on 18 December 1398. The city was sacked and left in ruins, and 100,000 or more captives are said to have been killed. In 1737 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> sacked Delhi after defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><p>In 1739 the Persian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> beat the Mughals at Karnal in less than three hours and then took Delhi. He sacked and looted the city, and a massacre of two days is said to have killed over 30,000 civilians. He carried off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a>, and left only after the emperor Muhammad Shah I begged for mercy and handed over the keys of the city and the royal treasury. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> sacked the city from 9 May to 4 June 1753, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>’s forces took it in 1757.</p><p>In the rebellion of 1857 mutineers reached Delhi on 11 May and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah Zafar</a> emperor. British forces laid siege to the city on 8 June and took it on 21 September, and it was significantly damaged. The last Mughal emperor was captured and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>. The anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> pogroms of 1984, set off by the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a>, killed around 2,800 people in Delhi according to government figures, and independent estimates are higher.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-fort"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-fort.jpg" alt="Red Fort" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delhi_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PerSona77, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> in the city, the Qutub Minar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>’s Tomb and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a>, all belong to the sultanate and Mughal periods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> recognises 1,200 heritage buildings and 175 monuments in Delhi as national heritage sites. A saying of the Northern Indo-Aryan languages, that Delhi is still far away, is used of a task far from finished.</p><p>The move of the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to Delhi was announced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> of 1911 and formally made on 12 December 1911. The new city received the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> in 1927 and was inaugurated on 13 February 1931. The project met opposition from the European business community of Calcutta, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>. The part built in the British period is informally known as Lutyens’ Delhi. New Delhi became the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947.</p><p>Delhi became a union territory in 1956, and the Constitution (Sixty-ninth Amendment) Act of 1991 named it the National Capital Territory. It contains New Delhi, the capital of India. The territory has some 17 million people, and over 11 million of them live in the city proper.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna<span>1921</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Delhi, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> river, was rebuilt in several successive cities, from a Tomara fort of 1052 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>’s Shahjahanabad and Lutyens’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, and is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</em></p>

